{"hash":"df898b95a96b423c","voice":"en-IN-NeerjaExpressiveNeural","rate":"+4%","dur":94.325,"said":"Look up at the shikhara. This temple is three shrines stacked one above another: Mahakaleshwar at the bottom, below the ground; Omkareshwar above him; and at the very top Nagchandreshwar, whose doors open only one day a year, on Nag Panchami. Now, the story of how Ujjain was saved. The Shiva Purana tells that long ago Ujjayini was ruled by King Chandrasena, a deep devotee of Mahakal. One of Shiva’s attendants gave him the Chintamani, a gem that could grant any wish. Other kings grew envious and surrounded the city with their armies. But Chandrasena did not lose heart; he went on worshipping Mahakal. Watching him was a little boy of five, the son of a cowherd woman. The child set up a pebble as his own linga and worshipped it with all his heart. His mother, angry, threw it away. The boy cried out to Shiva in grief, and where he had worshipped, a shining temple of Mahakala appeared. Hearing of it, the besieging kings gave up their hostility. Then Hanuman appeared, named the boy Shrikar, and foretold that eight generations later his line would bring forth Nanda, the foster-father of Krishna. Now climb the steps into the hall. Mahakal sits below the earth; the one who sits above him is waiting inside.\n\nDid you know? At Omkareshwar on the Narmada the order is reversed: there, Mahakaleshwar is on the floor above Omkareshwar.","segments":[{"key":"text","start":0,"end":1216},{"key":"didYouKnow","start":1232,"end":1340}],"words":[[0.098,0.252,0,4],[0.362,0.204,5,7],[0.579,0.12,8,10],[0.711,0.096,11,14],[0.819,0.577,15,23],[2.272,0.288,25,29],[2.572,0.481,30,36],[3.065,0.144,37,39],[3.221,0.288,40,45],[3.522,0.457,46,53],[3.99,0.385,54,61],[4.387,0.204,62,65],[4.603,0.288,66,71],[4.904,0.505,72,79],[5.673,0.889,81,94],[6.574,0.108,95,97],[6.695,0.084,98,101],[6.791,0.505,102,108],[7.536,0.312,110,115],[7.861,0.108,116,119],[7.981,0.493,120,126],[8.678,0.745,128,139],[9.435,0.